Ported (logic translated to Rust, not copied verbatim) to
crates/kgraf-graph/src/leases/schedule.rs, per ROADMAP Phase 1.6's build plan
(decision 0055) and the evidence file
docs/decisions/evidence/2026-09-24-reference-architectures.md ("The best idea in the
repo for kGraf").

What was taken: the **layered topological sort** structure of `_schedule_tool_calls` --
compute a dependency set per item, repeatedly pull every item whose dependencies are
already scheduled into one parallel batch, and when no item is ready (a dependency
cycle), break it deterministically by running the lowest remaining index alone. This
loop is about a dozen lines and is the entire reusable idea; the surrounding
State-key-mapping code (`_state_io_for_call`, `_keys_intersect`) is Haystack's own
`State` dataclass and was not ported, only its role as "how a call's read/write sets are
compared" was, generalized to kGraf's lease keys (file paths and hierarchical node
ids, decision 0050).

Deliberate deviation from upstream, stated because it changes where dependency
direction comes from:

  Haystack: "read-after-write forces a later batch, write-write creates no
  dependency." Direction comes from dataflow -- a tool's writes merge into a shared
  `State` dict, and a later call must see an earlier call's write, so direction is a
  correctness question with one right answer. A rare true cycle (a call that both
  reads and writes the same key, requested more than once) is broken by running the
  lowest remaining index alone.

  kGraf: a write lease is mutual exclusion, not a merge target. Any overlap
  between two items' read/write sets (`WorkFootprint::conflicts_with`) is a reason
  they must not share a layer, but there is no data flowing between them, so which one
  goes first is not a correctness question -- only a determinism one. The port
  therefore orders a conflicting pair by declaration index (`i < j` always produces
  the edge `i -> j`), never by which one "reads" or "writes". This makes the
  dependency graph acyclic by construction, a stronger guarantee than upstream's, so
  upstream's cycle tie-break becomes dead code here -- kept anyway, at zero cost, as
  the same defensive fallback upstream reaches for. Items with disjoint footprints
  still batch together exactly as upstream intends.

kGraf's admission step (`LeaseRegistry` in the same crate) is not part of the port:
Haystack's scheduler ends at producing batches, and kGraf adds layer-by-layer
admission into the write-lease registry on top of it (`leases::schedule::admit_layers`).
